  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1045 PM MDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nThe weak upper level trough that passed across the region today \nand gave us some beautiful cumulus clouds and a few rain showers \nhas moved off to the southeast. In its wake we will have a short \nwave upper level ridge build across New Mexico and that will keep \nour skies clear, our winds light and high temperatures Friday \nafternoon will run a few degrees above average.\n\nFor the weekend, our upper level flow will turn more toward the\nsouthwest. This southwest flow will begin to draw a little\nmoisture up from northern Mexico. The moisture both Saturday and\nSunday will be pretty meager with only the Gila region and\nlocations along the NM/AZ border having much of a chance for\nprecipitation. High temperatures both days will run 5 to 8 degrees\nabove average. Will El Paso see one more 90 degree day on Sunday;\nwe will be close.\n\nFor Monday and Tuesday all eyes turn toward another tropical \nsystem making landfall in west central Mexico. The deterministic \nmodels have struggled with just how much moisture will be drawn \nnorth into our region. Unlike the remnants of Hurricane Polo that \npounded the region last week, the remnants of Hurricane Simon \nwill have a much greater distance to travel to reach the southwest\nU.S. Right now it seems like most of the moisture either doesn't \nmake it this far north or is sweep to the east by an upper level \ntrough. We need to keep an eye on things in case the models trend \nin a wetter direction, but right now the first of next week will \nsee a chance for rain, but it is not looking too bad. High \ntemperatures both Monday and Tuesday will run a few degrees above \naverage east of the Rio Grande and a few degrees below average \nwest of the river. Things look drier and a little cooler for the \nmiddle part of next week.",
